  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/02-living-fossils/polypterus-mokelembembe-en/",
      "html": "<p>Polypterus mokelembembe was described in 2006 and is the smallest bichir. It grows up to 25 cm long.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/02-living-fossils/polypterus-mokelembembe-en/",
      "html": "<p>Origin: D.R. Congo. All 16 recent bichir species originate from Africa.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/02-living-fossils/polypterus-mokelembembe-en/",
      "html": "<p>P. mokelembembe inhabits smaller blackwater creeks in the Congo region.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/02-living-fossils/polypterus-mokelembembe-en/",
      "html": "<p>They feed readily on all frozen and live food as long as it is of animal origin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/02-living-fossils/polypterus-mokelembembe-en/",
      "html": "<p>Polypterus mokelembembe is peaceful towards all possible tankmates as long as they cannot be regarded as food. The fish do not like strong light.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/02-living-fossils/polypterus-mokelembembe-en/",
      "html": "<p>Males and females can be distinguished quite easily, as in all Polypterus. The anal fin of the males is more than double the size of the anal fin of the female.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/02-living-fossils/polypterus-mokelembembe-en/",
      "html": "<p>The male's anal fin is spread and looks like a bowl when the fish spawn. During mating the male swims closely side-by-side with the female and surrounds the female's anal fin region with the spread anal fin. The eggs, which are spawned free in the water, are thus collected in the male's anal fin, which ensures they become fertilized. Polypterus do not care for their brood. The juveniles have external gills and look very much like newts.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/02-living-fossils/polypterus-mokelembembe-en/",
      "html": "<p>Formerly the species was confused with P. retropinnis. Lexicon: Polypterus: ancient Greek, means \"with many fins\", referring to the numerous dorsal finlets. mokelembembe: name of a legendary monster with a dinosaur-like appearance said to live in the Congo, comparable with Nessie from Loch Ness. The name was chosen as an allusion to the old age of bichirs, which existed already at the times of dinosaurs. retropinnis: Latin, means \"with fins turned backwards\".</p>"
    }
  ],
